				\begin{document}$$MCC=\frac{\left(TP\times TN\right)-\left(FP\times FN\right)}{\sqrt{\left(TP+FP\right)\left(TP+FN\right)\left(FP+TN\right)\left(TN+FN\right)}}$$\end{document}MCC=TP×TN−FP×FNTP+FPTP+FNFP+TNTN+FNWhere TP represents the count of positive samples that are correctly identified, TN denotes the count of negative samples accurately classified, FP indicates the count of negative samples misclassified as positive, and FN indicates to the count of positive samples mistakenly labeled as negative.

Red blood cells (RBCs) from rabbits were used for this test. Hemolytic activity of the peptides was measured by first mixing the RBCs with the peptide solutions (n = 5). The mixtures were then incubated at 37 °C for 1 h. Afterwards, the samples were centrifuged at 1500 rpm for 8 min. The optical density of the supernatant was detected at 570 nm using a microplate reader. RBCs in PBS were used as the negative control, and 0.5% (w/v) TritonX-100-treated RBCs were used as the positive control. The percentage of hemolysis was calculated as Eq. (5).5\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
